    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d709">

                <pb facs="0858" n="840" sameAs="frus:frus1946v11/pg840"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.24522/2–246: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Ecuador</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Scotten</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">restricted</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Quito</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-02-02T11:00:00-05:00">February 2, 1946—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 6:25 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>56. Shaw<anchor n="12" xml:id="d709-f1"/> and I attended the ceremony of the
                    turning over of the base at Salinas yesterday to the Ecuadoran authorities.
                    General Crittenberger,<anchor n="13" xml:id="d709-f2"/> two general officers and
                    an Admiral from the Canal Zone as well as the Ecuadoran Minister of
                        Defense,<anchor n="14" xml:id="d709-f3"/> many high Ecuadoran officials and
                    a representative from the Foreign Office were present. The ceremony went off
                    smoothly and the Minister for Defense expressed very clearly the appreciation of
                    his Govt for the reception of all the equipment on the base. A luncheon followed
                    for General Crittenberger at which he and General Homer<anchor n="15"
                        xml:id="d709-f4"/> received decorations from the Ecuadoran Govt. We took to
                    Salinas the press representative of IIS [<hi rendition="#italic">INS?</hi>] who
                    flew back a detailed account of the ceremony and an excellent photographic
                    record. Both the photographs and news account are published in all the Quito
                    newspapers of this morning and I feel that this turnover has been given adequate
                    and very favorable publicity from our point of view.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Scotten</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d709-f1" n="12" xml:id="d709-f1-content">
                <p>George P. Shaw, Counselor of Embassy.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d709-f2" n="13" xml:id="d709-f2-content">
                <p>Lt. Gen. Willis D. Crittenberger, Commanding General, Caribbean Defense
                    Command.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d709-f3" n="14" xml:id="d709-f3-content">
                <p>Col. Carlos Macheno.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d709-f4" n="15" xml:id="d709-f4-content">
                <p>Maj. Gen. John L. Homer, Deputy Commander, Panama Canal Department.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
